name: proving this machine
boss: Void
flavor: Nothing you deleted is as gone as you think.
brief: Everything so far was about using a machine. This is about interrogating
brief: one -- and the machine you know least about is the one you are standing
brief: on right now.
brief:
brief: xos makes a specific claim: every block of the root filesystem is
brief: checked against a hash tree whose root hash arrives on the kernel
brief: command line, inside an image signed with keys you enrolled yourself.
brief: You are about to check that claim by hand, with commands you already
brief: know. No special tool. Nothing you could not do on someone else's box.
---
q: what command line was this kernel booted with
a: cat /proc/cmdline
ref: cat
teach: cat
---
q: pull just the verity root hash out of that command line
a: sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: it is the 64 hex characters that follow the word sha256
---
q: what filesystem is mounted at / and with what flags
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $3, $4}' /proc/mounts
out: .
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: confirm the root is mounted read-only
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $4}' /proc/mounts | grep -o '^ro'
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
---
q: what device is the root actually on
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $1}' /proc/mounts
out: .
ref: awk
teach: awk
---
q: hash the bytes of the filesystem you are standing on
a: sha256sum /dev/dm-0
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum
hint: dm-0 is the device dm-verity hands you once it is checking every block
---
q: %show% just that digest, with the device name cut off
a: sha256sum /dev/dm-0 | cut -d' ' -f1
ref: cut
teach: cut -d
teach: cut -f
---
q: confirm the kernel was told to panic rather than continue on a bad block
a: grep -o panic_on_corruption /proc/cmdline
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
hint: fail closed. a verified system that carries on after a mismatch is not one
---
q: what does the kernel say about verity in its boot log
a: dmesg | grep -i verity
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg
---
q: is the kernel locked down, and how far
a: cat /sys/kernel/security/lockdown
ref: cat
teach: cat
---
q: prove there is no module loader to insert code into this kernel
a: [ -e /proc/modules ] && echo present || echo absent
ref: [
teach: [ -e
---
q: prove /dev/mem is not there to read or write physical memory through
a: [ -e /dev/mem ] && echo present || echo absent
ref: [
teach: [ -e
---
q: prove the root filesystem refuses to be remounted writable
a: mount -o remount,rw / 2>/dev/null && echo WRITABLE || echo refused
ref: mount
teach: mount -o
---
q: prove nothing dropped in your home directory can be executed
a: cp /bin/busybox $HOME/x && chmod +x $HOME/x && $HOME/x true && echo ran || echo refused
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: $HOME is on a tmpfs mounted noexec
---
q: %count% how many trust anchors are compiled into the tls binary
a: strings /bin/tlstunnel | grep -c 'Internet Security Research Group'
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: strings
teach: strings
---
q: %list% every command this system documents, and count them
a: learn list | wc -l
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: learn
teach: learn
